      "text": "To find the distance between two points \\((x_1, y_1)\\) and \\((x_2, y_2)\\) in a coordinate plane, we use the distance formula:\n\\[\nd = \\sqrt{(x_2 - x_1)^2 + (y_2 - y_1)^2}\n\\]\n\nGiven the points \\((-3, -4)\\) and \\((4, -5)\\), we can substitute \\(x_1 = -3\\), \\(y_1 = -4\\), \\(x_2 = 4\\), and \\(y_2 = -5\\) into the formula.\n\nFirst, calculate the difference in the \\(x\\)-coordinates:\n\\[\nx_2 - x_1 = 4 - (-3) = 4 + 3 = 7\n\\]\n\nNext, calculate the difference in the \\(y\\)-coordinates:\n\\[\ny_2 - y_1 = -5 - (-4) = -5 + 4 = -1\n\\]\n\nNow, square these differences:\n\\[\n(x_2 - x_1)^2 = 7^2 = 49\n\\]\n\\[\n(y_2 - y_1)^2 = (-1)^2 = 1\n\\]\n\nAdd these squared differences:\n\\[\n(x_2 - x_1)^2 + (y_2 - y_1)^2 = 49 + 1 = 50\n\\]\n\nFinally, take the square root of the sum to find the distance:\n\\[\nd = \\sqrt{50} = \\sqrt{25 \\times 2} = 5\\sqrt{2}\n\\]\n\nTherefore, the distance between the points \\((-3, -4)\\) and \\((4, -5)\\) is \\(\\boxed{5\\sqrt{2}}\\).",
